import{H as e,at as t,it as n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.dmHZ7NHG.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"The cipher & the strand","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"params":{"slug":"cipher","address":"f6bb27ac-9cc6-8ee3-8b45-a016b19714ba","receipt":"091aaddc-ca90-8692-9c4a-0c714b4a462f"},"relativePath":"docs/publications/cipher.md","filePath":"docs/publications/[slug].md"}`),a={name:`docs/publications/cipher.md`};function o(e,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="the-cipher-the-strand" tabindex="-1">The cipher &amp; the strand <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cipher-the-strand" aria-label="Permalink to “The cipher &amp; the strand”">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>crypto ∩ DNA, honest by construction — base-pairing is a fixed-key XOR (a one-time-pad step), the pad is self-inverse but key reuse leaks the plaintext XOR (why a step must rotate), a linear fold is malleable (a receipt is integrity, not a seal), the transport leaks message length, translation is lossy (never a cipher), an affine S-box is invertible but linear, and Grover only halves the key (256→128, not a break) — the shared algebra and its honest limits</p></blockquote><p>These 11 facts hold by decision. Each was proven in Lean 4 — no Mathlib, checked sorry-free — and every sentence below links the proof that seals it. This note claims nothing beyond what its theorems settle: not that the domain is complete, only that the prose says nothing the proofs do not. It was written by reading the sealed ledger; read the proofs, not the prose.</p><h2 id="the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" tabindex="-1">The facts, each backed by its proof <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" aria-label="Permalink to “The facts, each backed by its proof”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Base-pairing is a self-inverse map: the complement comp(x)=3−x applied twice is the identity (A↔T↔A, C↔G↔C) — a decrypt that equals its encrypt, like the diamond reflection. — <a href="/theorem/dna_complement_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>The complement has NO fixed point — no base pairs with itself — so, like a good permutation cipher, it moves every symbol. — <a href="/theorem/dna_complement_fixed_point_free">proof</a>.</li><li>Base-pairing IS a XOR cipher: on the 2-bit encoding comp(x)=3−x equals x XOR 3 — a one-time-pad STEP with the fixed pad 3. Real, but a FIXED pad is public, not secret. — <a href="/theorem/complement_is_xor_key3">proof</a>.</li><li>The one-time-pad is its own inverse (Vernam): (m ⊕ k) ⊕ k = m for every symbol and key — the one information-theoretically secure primitive, WHEN the key is fresh and never reused. — <a href="/theorem/otp_self_inverse">proof</a>.</li><li>Key reuse is fatal: two messages under the SAME key leak their plaintext XOR — (m₁⊕k) ⊕ (m₂⊕k) = m₁⊕m₂, independent of k. The honest reason a step MUST advance (the ratchet), and why a fixed-pad complement hides nothing. — <a href="/theorem/otp_key_reuse_leaks_xor">proof</a>.</li><li>A linear (XOR) fold is malleable: flipping the input by d flips the fold by exactly d — (a⊕d)⊕a = d — so it binds nothing an adversary cannot adjust. A content-address is INTEGRITY/routing, NOT a binding one-way seal. — <a href="/theorem/xor_fold_is_malleable">proof</a>.</li><li>The uuid transport leaks SIZE: a message of b bits occupies ⌈b/115⌉ uuids, a step function of length — content is hidden by the cipher, message LENGTH is not (the chain grows in whole-uuid quanta of 115 bits). — <a href="/theorem/transport_leaks_length">proof</a>.</li><li>The genetic code reads bases three at a time: 4³ = 64 codons — the DNA alphabet cubed, the domain the code maps from. — <a href="/theorem/codons_four_cubed">proof</a>.</li><li>Translation is LOSSY, never a cipher: 64 codons map onto only 21 outcomes (20 amino acids + stop), and 64 &gt; 21, so by pigeonhole the map cannot be injective — a hash-like reduction that cannot be inverted, not encryption. — <a href="/theorem/translation_is_lossy">proof</a>.</li><li>An affine substitution E(x)=2x+3 over ℤ/5 is a bijection — it hits every residue, so it is an invertible S-box (unlike lossy translation). But it is LINEAR, hence weak: two known plaintext pairs recover it. Invertible ≠ secure. — <a href="/theorem/affine_is_permutation">proof</a>.</li><li>The honest quantum posture: Grover’s search is a QUADRATIC speedup, not a break — a 2n-bit key space costs ~2ⁿ work ((2ⁿ)² = 2²ⁿ), so a 256-bit key falls to ~128-bit, still strong. Symmetric-only means no Shor target at all. — <a href="/theorem/grover_quadratic_bound">proof</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="provenance" tabindex="-1">Provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#provenance" aria-label="Permalink to “Provenance”">​</a></h2><p>These 11 proofs fold, order-invariant, to the receipt <code>091aaddc-ca90-8692-9c4a-0c714b4a462f</code> — recompute it from the same ledger and it returns. The note itself content-addresses to a uuid, so any edit is visible. Writing descends from reading: this note holds only what the proofs it links already sealed, and it was audited by uuidna&#39;s own honesty gate before it was published — a sentence that overreached a proof would have been refused, not shipped.</p><p>Source · <a href="https://github.com/uuidna/uuidna/blob/main/lean/Cipher.lean" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">lean/Cipher.lean</a> · <a href="/theorems">all theorems</a></p><hr><p><strong>Audited before published</strong> · this note passed uuidna&#39;s honesty gate at build time — every claim above links the proof that seals it. Content-address <code>f6bb27ac</code> · proofs fold to receipt <code>091aaddc</code> (the handles; the full uuids are in this page&#39;s meta, recomputable) · 11 sealed theorems.</p><p><a href="/publications">All publications</a> · <a href="/theorems">All theorems</a> · <a href="/trials">How auditing works</a></p>`,11)]])}var s=e(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};